    "STYLE",
    "\n  <local-path> Both faces are served from beside this page, so Chrome shapes the same\n     font binaries the repo ships. A fixture that leaned on an installed system\n     font would record advances that only reproduce on the machine that\n     captured it. */\n  @font-face {\n    font-family: 'Pulp Fixture Sans';\n    src: url('Inter-Regular.ttf') format('truetype');\n    font-weight: 400;\n    font-style: normal;\n  }\n  <local-path> One variable file covering 300-800, so 400 and 700 are real instances\n     rather than a synthesised smear of one master. */\n  @font-face {\n    font-family: 'Pulp Fixture Variable';\n    src: url('FunnelDisplay-VariableFont_wght.ttf') format('truetype');\n    font-weight: 300 800;\n    font-style: normal;\n  }\n  html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #ffffff; }\n  body { width: 640px; height: 360px; position: relative; }\n  .run {\n    position: absolute;\n    left: 24px;\n    color: #101010;\n    font-family: 'Pulp Fixture Sans';\n    font-size: 16px;\n    line-height: 20px;\n  }\n  <local-path> Narrower than its text, so Chrome breaks it into several line boxes whose\n     bounds are all narrower than the block. */\n  #wrapped { top: 24px; width: 220px; white-space: normal; }\n  <local-path> The same text in the same block, forbidden to break. One line box, whose\n     width exceeds the block's. */\n  #unwrapped { top: 140px; width: 220px; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .weight {\n    position: absolute;\n    left: 24px;\n    color: #101010;\n    font-family: 'Pulp Fixture Variable';\n    font-size: 20px;\n    line-height: 24px;\n    white-space: nowrap;\n  }\n  <local-path> Identical text, identical size, one weight apart. The advance difference\n     between these two line boxes is the whole bold-measurement question. */\n  #regular { top: 220px; font-weight: 400; }\n  #bold { top: 260px; font-weight: 700; }\n  <local-path> An inline span splits one visual paragraph into three sibling runs. The\n     run AFTER the span resumes mid-line before returning to the left edge, so\n     its layout box is the union of lines it shares with its siblings and no\n     single box describes where its text goes. Non-ASCII on purpose: the line\n     offsets Chrome reports are UTF-16 code units while the text is UTF-8. */\n  #inline { position: absolute; left: 320px; top: 24px; width: 240px;\n            color: #101010; font-family: 'Pulp Fixture Sans'; font-size: 16px;\n            line-height: 20px; white-space: normal; }\n  #inline b { font-weight: 400; color: #b03030; }\n",
